course: everyday
lesson: 51
title: Manners and kindness
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Manners are small habits that show other people you respect them: saying please and thank you, waiting for your turn, saying sorry and listening without interrupting.
explain: They matter because every person has feelings: a kind word makes someone feel noticed, while being ignored, pushed or interrupted makes them feel unimportant.
explain: Taking turns is fair: when everyone waits for their go, everyone gets a go, and nobody needs to push or shout.
explain: Good listening means looking at the person, letting them finish, and then answering about what they actually said.
explain: In a school lunch line in Texas, Maya holds the door for the boy behind her; he says thank you and smiles, and two tiny acts of kindness leave both of them a little happier.
explain: A boy in Ohio laughed at a new girl's accent every day; she started to dread school and cried at home, and it took her months to feel happy there again.
explain: A girl in Leeds pushed past people to get on a crowded bus first; she knocked an older lady, who fell and hurt her wrist.
fact: Saying please turns an order into a polite request.
fact: Saying thank you shows that you noticed what someone did for you.
fact: When someone thanks you, the polite reply is you are welcome.
fact: Saying sorry when you hurt or bump someone shows you care about how they feel.
fact: Taking turns means everyone gets a fair chance.
fact: Good listeners look at the speaker and let them finish before they talk.
fact: Interrupting tells the speaker that what they say does not matter to you.
fact: If you need to speak while others are talking and it is not an emergency, you wait for a pause and say excuse me.
fact: Manners matter with everyone: family, friends, strangers, shop workers and drivers.
fact: Kindness often spreads, because people who are treated kindly are more likely to be kind to others.
fact: Good manners at the table include chewing with your mouth closed and not grabbing food across other people.
fact: A good winner says good game, and a good loser congratulates the winner.
fact: Unkind words can hurt someone for a long time, much longer than a scratch.
fact: Pushing and shoving in crowds can knock people over and injure them.
fact: Families differ: some children have two mums or two dads, and that is normal.
fact: Questions about someone's private life, such as when they will get married, can upset them, so it is kind to change the subject.
fact: When grown-ups say someone is on their own again, it usually means their partner has left or they have split up; the kind thing is to be friendly and not gossip.
exercise: What does saying please show when you ask for something? | That you are asking politely, not giving an order | That the other person must give it to you at once | That you are sorry for asking at all | That the thing already belongs to you
exercise: Your aunt gives you a present you do not really like. What is the kind thing to do? | Thank her for thinking of you | Tell her you do not like it | Hand it straight back to her | Put it away without a word
exercise: Your friend is telling you about her weekend. What does good listening look like? | Looking at her and letting her finish | Looking at your tablet while she talks | Starting your own story in the middle of hers | Guessing the end of her story out loud
exercise: Why do children take turns on the playground slide? | So that everyone gets a fair go | So that the biggest children always go first | So that the slide does not wear out | So that the teacher can count the children
exercise: You bump into someone by accident in the corridor. What do you say? | Sorry, are you all right? | Watch where you are going! | Nothing, because it was an accident | It was your fault, not mine
exercise: You want to ask your mum something that is not urgent, but she is talking to a neighbour. What do you do? | Wait for a pause and say excuse me | Pull her sleeve until she stops talking | Talk louder than both of them | Ask the neighbour to go home
exercise: Why are manners important? | They show respect and help people get along | They are rules that only grown-ups need | They only matter when a teacher is watching | They make you more important than others
exercise: What is a kind act? | Doing something helpful or friendly for someone else | Helping only when you know you will get a reward or a prize | Doing what you want and ignoring others | Being polite only to your best friends
exercise: A new boy drops all his books in the hall. What is the kind thing to do? | Help him pick them up | Laugh because it looks funny | Step around the books and keep going | Tell the whole class what happened
exercise: What do you say when someone holds a door open for you? | Thank you | Excuse me | You are welcome | Please
exercise: Why can one kind act lead to more kindness? | People treated kindly often feel like being kind to others | Kindness only spreads when adults order it | People must pay back every kind act at once, or it stops working | Kindness only spreads inside one family
exercise: Why do we chew with our mouths closed at the table? | So others do not have to see the food in our mouths | So that the food tastes much sweeter | So that we can eat a lot faster | So that the food in our mouths stays warm for much longer
exercise: When do you say you are welcome? | When someone thanks you | When someone asks you for help | When someone says sorry to you | When you meet someone for the first time
exercise: Is it good manners to thank a bus driver or a shop worker? | Yes, everyone who helps you deserves thanks | No, because they are only doing their job | Only if you know them by name | Only if they thank you first
test: In a library in Dublin, a girl needs to get past a man reading at a shelf. What does she say? | Excuse me, please | Move, I need to get past | Nothing, she just squeezes past him | Hey, you are standing in my way
test: Which is true? | Manners matter with everyone, not just with teachers | Manners matter only when an adult is watching | Good manners mean always agreeing with everyone you talk to | Manners are needed only at school and at parties
test: Two children both want the last free swing. What is the fairest way? | Take turns, each swinging for the same count | The faster runner keeps it for the whole break | The older child gets it for being older | Nobody gets it, so the swing stays empty
test: What would probably happen if nobody in a class ever took turns to speak? | Everyone would talk at once and nobody would be heard | The lesson would go faster and better | The quiet children would be heard much more often than before | The teacher would have nothing left to do
test: Grandpa starts telling a story you have heard before. What is the kind thing to do? | Listen and let him enjoy telling it | Tell him the story is boring | Shout out the ending before he gets there | Turn on the TV while he is talking
test: Your friend lends you a pencil for a test. What is the polite way to return it? | Give it back and say thanks for lending it | Keep it, since they gave it to you | Drop it on their desk without a single word | Give it back only if they ask for it
test: Why do we look at people while they talk to us? | It shows we are paying attention to them | It makes their voice sound much clearer | It stops them from talking for too long | It lets us check if they are lying
test: Which of these is NOT good manners? | Interrupting someone in the middle of a sentence | Saying please when you ask for the salt | Holding a door open for the person behind you | Saying sorry when you bump into someone
test: A boy in Auckland wins a board game against his cousins. What shows good manners? | Saying good game to everyone who played | Cheering loudly in the losers' faces | Telling the others they played badly | Packing the game away without a word
test: gimme the ketchup | Please can you pass the ketchup? | Ketchup, now | Give it here | Just reach across and grab it, it's quicker
test: bumped into a lady at the shop | Say sorry | Keep walking | Laugh it off | Tell her to watch out
test: mum's chatting, i need to ask something, not urgent | Wait for a pause and say excuse me | Shout over her | Pull her arm hard | Keep talking louder and louder until she stops
test: lost the game, what do i say | Well played, to the winner | It was unfair | I'll never play again | Nothing, just walk off
test: everyone laughs when i copy the new kid's accent, it's banter | Stop, it hurts him even if others laugh | Keep going, he will get used to it in the end | Do it only when he cannot hear | Do it once more then stop
test: bus is packed, gonna shove to get a seat | Wait your turn, shoving can knock someone over | Shove, everyone does it, that's how buses work | Push only the grown-ups | Push but say sorry after
test: Max says saying sorry is for losers. right? | No, saying sorry helps fix things after a mistake | Yes, it shows you are weak | Yes, people forget anyway | Yes, unless an adult stands there and makes you say it
test: everyone's saying the new girl's rude cos she didn't say thank you when Ms Lee gave her the book | Do not spread it; she may be shy or new, and labelling her is unkind | Spread it, because one time proves it | Spread it, so she learns manners | Spread it, and say she was rude twice and rolled her eyes at Ms Lee
test: my mate says manners are only for grown-ups and teachers, not for shop workers or bus drivers | Wrong; manners matter with everyone, shop workers and drivers too | He is right, only teachers count | He is right, workers are paid so manners do not matter at all to them | He is right, manners are for family only
test: heard Ollie said sorry to the kid he bumped and now people say he's soft, lol | Saying sorry shows he cares; calling him soft is unkind and wrong | They are right, sorry shows weakness | They are right, he should have ignored it and walked off like nothing happened | They are right, only babies say sorry
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the effect of saying «please»? | It converts an order into a polite request | It obliges the listener to comply at once | It signifies that the speaker is angry | It has no effect upon the meaning
exam: It being given that a child must speak whilst others converse and no emergency exists, select the appropriate conduct. | To await a pause and say «excuse me» | To speak loudly over the others | To tug at an adult's sleeve repeatedly | To leave the room without a word
exam: Identify the principal message conveyed by interrupting a speaker. | That what the speaker says is of no importance to the listener | That the listener agrees entirely | That the listener is especially polite | That the speaker should speak more loudly and more slowly so as to be understood
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning kindness. | Kindness tends to spread, those treated kindly being more likely to be kind | Kindness is quickly forgotten and has no effect | Kindness causes others to behave more unkindly, since they take advantage of it | Kindness affects only the person who is kind
test: grandad asked nosy question at dinner, "so when's Auntie Jo getting married then", she went all quiet | He asked when she will marry her partner; it is private, so change the subject kindly | He asked about a party, so ask again louder | It means she is in trouble, so laugh | It means she is already married and nobody told grandad, so congratulate her
test: new girl's got two mums n Ben said "that's weird" right in front of her | Some families have two mums and that is normal; say kindly that families differ | Agree it is weird so Ben likes you | Ask her lots of questions about her two mums in front of the whole class | Tell her she is not allowed two mums
test: heard nan say "she's on her own again, poor love" bout the lady next door | Her partner has left or they have split up; be friendly to her and do not gossip | It means she is lost, so call the police | It means she is alone in the house right now, so go round and let yourself in | It means she is unkind, so stop saying hello
